      "message": "For 1, you can use ztest hooks to do the cleanup:\n\n----\n\nYes, using a ZTEST post-test hook is actually a much better approach than putting it at the end of the test function. That is an excellent suggestion!\n\nHere is why: **Assertion Failures Abort the Function.** \nIf you put the teardown code at the end of `test_lock_flash_control_register`, and one of your `zassert_*` macros fails in the middle of the test, the test function will immediately abort. That means your manual teardown would be skipped, and the flash would still be left permanently unlocked for the rest of the suite. \n\nUsing a ZTEST `after` hook guarantees that the teardown code executes regardless of whether the test passes or fails.\n\n### How to implement it\n\nYou can implement this by replacing the `NULL` in your `ZTEST_SUITE` definition with an `after` callback. \n\nFirst, define the teardown function. It will run after every test in the `flash_physical` suite:\n\n```c\nstatic void flash_physical_after(void *data)\n{\n#if defined(CONFIG_SOC_FAMILY_NPCX)\n\tconst struct device *dev \u003d DEVICE_DT_GET(DT_CHOSEN(cros_ec_flash_controller));\n\t\n\tif (device_is_ready(dev)) {\n\t\t/* Guarantee flash is locked down after the test completes or fails */\n\t\tcros_flash_npcx_set_write_disable(dev);\n\t}\n#endif\n}\n```\n\nThen, update your suite definition at the top of the file to wire up the hook (it is the 5th parameter):\n\n```c\n/* \n * ZTEST_SUITE parameters: \n * suite_name, predicate, setup, before, after, teardown\n */\nZTEST_SUITE(flash_physical, NULL, NULL, NULL, flash_physical_after, NULL);\n```",
